<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Arts and Crafts – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> This Is a Historical Building.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe a favorite hobby. Interactive: Role-play everyday situations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a favorite craft or art activity (e.g., "My favorite craft is making baskets. First, you prepare the materials and soak the fibers"). Can role-play conversations about buying art (e.g., "I'd like to buy this tagua carving. How much does it cost?"). Can discuss local art in a conversation with peers (e.g., "This artist has created beautiful sculptures").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of famous historical buildings in Panama (e.g., Casco Antiguo, Panama Canal Administration Building) and various Panamanian crafts (molas, tagua carvings, woven baskets). Students chorally name the items they recognize. Teacher asks, "What kind of art or craft can you find in or near these buildings?" encouraging short answers. This activates vocabulary like <i>building, mola, tagua carving, basket, craft</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing one of the crafts using target grammar and vocabulary. "Look at this <i>tagua carving</i>. An <i>artist</i> has <i>created</i> this <i>unique sculpture</i> from a seed. It is very <i>detailed</i>." Teacher points to the words as they say them, emphasizing syllable stress on <i>sculpture</i> (/skʌlp.tʃər/) and <i>design</i> (/dɪ'zaɪn/) and articulation of '-ed' endings (e.g., 'created').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <i>sculpture</i> something you wear or something you look at?" (Expected answer: "Look at.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What material has the artist used for the <i>carving</i>?" (Expected answer: "Tagua seed.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue between two friends visiting an art <i>exhibition</i> inside a historical <i>building</i>. The dialogue includes descriptions of artworks and an artist's achievements, using Present Perfect and adjectives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Excerpt:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Wow, this <i>gallery</i> is amazing! The <i>building</i> itself is so old and beautiful. Have you ever been here before?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Luis:</b> "No, this is my first time. Look at this <i>mural</i>! The <i>artist</i> has <i>created</i> such a <i>vibrant</i> and <i>colorful</i> piece. It tells a whole story."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Yes, it’s truly <i>unique</i>. I read that this <i>artist</i> also <i>sculpts</i>. She <i>has created</i> many <i>bold sculptures</i> for public spaces too. Yesterday, we visited an <i>artist</i> who makes <i>molas</i>, she told us about her process. She said, 'First, you prepare the fabric, then you cut out the shapes.'"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing creations ("The building is a two-story structure, with a rectangular design and white stone facades."); Describing achievements of artists ("This artist has created beautiful sculptures."); Describing how to create something ("First, you prepare the materials and soak the fibers.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral):</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where are Ana and Luis?" (Gist: In an art gallery/exhibition.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of art does Luis like in the gallery?" (Detail: The mural, described as vibrant and colorful.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What other craft does Ana mention?" (Detail: Molas.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice speaking using Present Perfect, Present and Past Simple, and Imperatives to describe art, artists' achievements, and creation processes. Focus on vocabulary: <i>artist, craft, basket, sculpture, gallery, studio, exhibition, canvas, building, mural, mola, tagua carving, create, paint, sculpt, design, carve, display, creative, artistic, handmade, colorful, abstract, bold, vibrant, minimalist, timeless, unique, detailed</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> Teacher provides sentence beginnings on the board, and students complete them orally in pairs using target vocabulary and grammar. Each pair takes turns completing. For example: "This <i>artist</i> ______ (create) many <i>vibrant murals</i>." (Student A: "This artist *has created* many vibrant murals."). "To make a <i>basket</i>, first you ______ (prepare) the materials." (Student B: "To make a basket, first you *prepare* the materials."). Focus on present perfect and imperatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture Description Chain Drill:</b> Students work in small groups. The teacher shows a picture of an art piece or a historical building (e.g., a <i>mola</i>, a <i>tagua carving</i>, a building with a <i>mural</i>). One student starts by describing it using one target adjective (e.g., "This <i>mola</i> is <i>colorful</i>."). The next student adds another adjective or detail using a different tense (e.g., "An <i>artist has created</i> it <i>skillfully</i>."). The third student adds another (e.g., "It has a <i>unique design</i>."). The fourth describes a step to create it using an imperative (e.g., "First, <i>cut out</i> the fabric."). Teacher monitors pronunciation of -ed, -ing endings and syllable stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Explain Your Favorite Craft" Pair Interview:</b> Students interview their partner about a favorite craft or art activity (real or imagined). They use questions like: "What is your favorite <i>craft</i>?" "How do you <i>create</i> it? First, you...?" "Have you ever <i>displayed</i> your work?" Partners share their answers focusing on using descriptive adjectives and imperatrives. E.g., "My favorite <i>craft</i> is making <i>baskets</i>. First, you <i>prepare</i> the materials and <i>soak</i> the fibers. Then, you <i>weave</i> them <i>skillfully</i>."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-play – "Art Fair at a Historical Site".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Task:</b> Students work in groups of three. They imagine they are at an art fair held within the grounds of a historical building. One student is an <i>artist</i> or vendor, describing their <i>craft</i>. Two students are visitors, interested in buying or learning about the art. The 'artist' describes their favorite <i>craft</i>, how they <i>create</i> it (using imperatives), and what makes it <i>unique</i>. The 'visitors' ask questions about the art, the <i>artist's achievements</i> (using Present Perfect), and the price. They can discuss whether they want to buy a <i>tagua carving</i> or a <i>mola</i>. They also discuss the historical <i>building</i> itself, using adjectives to describe its appearance. Each student must speak for at least 1 minute.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Snippets:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Artist:</b> "Welcome! My name is Maria, and I <i>create handmade molas</i>. This one is very <i>colorful</i> and has a <i>bold design</i>. I <i>have created</i> molas for ten years. First, you <i>choose</i> your fabrics, then you <i>cut out</i> the geometric shapes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Visitor 1:</b> "That's beautiful! I saw another <i>artist</i> who <i>has created</i> some amazing <i>sculptures</i> from wood. How much does this <i>mola</i> cost?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Visitor 2:</b> "I love how this <i>gallery</i> is inside such an old <i>building</i>. It makes the art feel so <i>timeless</i>. Has this <i>artist displayed</i> her work here before?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Sociolinguistic Competence:</b> Propose and develop steps for art projects in class while engaging in feedback exchanges (e.g., "Start the mola by drawing geometric shapes, then cut out the fabric.").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play, focusing on oral production.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Art Fair at a Historical Site" role-play in their groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target vocabulary (<i>sculpture, design</i>) and inflectional endings (-ed, -ing) clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporates at least 5-7 words from the lesson's vocabulary (e.g., <i>artist, craft, mola, tagua carving, unique, create, building, colorful</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Successfully uses Present Perfect (e.g., "This artist has created..."), Present Simple (e.g., "My favorite craft is..."), and Imperatives (e.g., "First, you prepare...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Communicates ideas clearly with reasonable pauses and attempts to maintain conversation flow.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively participates in the group, responds appropriately to peers, and engages in the role-play.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today to describe art is ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to explain how to make a craft because ______."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral or written short answers):</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of talking about art or crafts was most fun for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you want to practice more to improve your speaking about art?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The role-play activity worked well in encouraging students to use the target vocabulary and grammar in an interactive scenario. Most students engaged in describing creations and asking questions. Some struggled with spontaneous use of the Present Perfect, often defaulting to Past Simple. Next time, I need to provide more explicit sentence frames for Present Perfect use during the practice stage. Scaffolding could include a mini-dialogue prompt card with blanks for different tenses for weaker groups. I also need to ensure consistent monitoring of pronunciation of inflectional endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The speaking practice from today, especially describing art and artists' achievements, will directly lead into Lesson 4 (Writing Composition). Students will use the same vocabulary and grammar to write descriptions of art pieces or short biographies of artists, building on their oral fluency.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>